  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 140 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cold air damming regime likely to persist into the \nafternoon, with another day of cool temperatures and light rain. \n\nToday should be the last day of the CAD wedge allowing for cool, wet \nconditions over central NC. A mid level low looks to slowly move \nover central NC through the day today. This will allow for forcing \nfor continued light rain/drizzle in mainly the west and south this \nmorning through this afternoon. Then, dry air should move in from \nthe northwest, helping erode the CAD regime this afternoon into \ntonight as the low shifts offshore. With the increased cloud cover \ninto at least early afternoon, another day of below average \ntemperatures are expected. Highs this afternoon look to reach the \nlow 60s in the northwest to around 70 in the east. Temperatures \ncould also remain cooler if the CAD regime takes longer to dissipate \nthis afternoon. Overnight, lows should dip into the upper 40s in the \nnorthwest to the mid 50s in the southeast. \n\nAdditionally, a surface level low pressure system will continue to \nstrengthen and deepen off the NC coast through the day today. This \nwill allow for the surface pressure gradient to strengthen, allowing \nfor gusty winds across the region. 20-25 mph gusts are most likely, \nbut could reach up to 30 mph, especially as you go further east.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A period of autumnal weather on Friday and Saturday \nwith the coolest low temperatures since early June if not mid May \nwill give way to a warming trend with highs climbing into the upper \n80s by Wednesday. \n\nDeep and anomalously strong low pressure off the mid-Atlantic coast \non Friday will drive a chilly and dry airmass into the region. Low-\nlevel thickness values on Friday morning will range in the 1350s. \nMorning dew points in the 40s may mix out into the upper 30s with \ndeep afternoon mixing. It should be a beautiful fall day with mostly \nsunny skies and highs around 70. The coolest morning since the first \nweek of June if not mid May is expected on Saturday with lows \nranging in the mid 40s to around 50. These lows range about 6 to 11 \ndegrees below average. \n\nThe airmass on Saturday will moderate with morning thickness values \nwarming 10 to 15m yielding highs in the mid 70s in most locations \nwith mostly sunny skies. Lows on Sunday morning will generally range \nin the upper 40s to lower 50s although if radiational cooling \nconditions are maximized, lows could be cooler and more similar to \nSaturday morning. The mid and upper level flow will transition to \nmore northwesterly on Sunday and Monday with highs moderating into \nthe upper 70s to lower 80s which is a few degrees above average. \n\nThe flow will become increasingly southwesterly on Tuesday and \nWednesday with morning low level thickness values warming into the \n1390s on Wednesday morning and near 1400m on Thursday morning. These \nvalues will support highs mainly in the lower to mid 80s on Tuesday \nand in the mid to upper 80s on Wednesday which is about 6 to 10 \ndegrees above average. Lows will moderate through the period and \nrange in the lower to mid 60s by Wednesday night bringing a return \nof some mugginess to the area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Another extended period of dry weather is expected \nfrom Friday through Wednesday with limited rain chances arriving \ntoward the end of the work week. \n\nA dry north to northwesterly flow aloft from Friday through early \nnext week will result in fair weather and the maintenance of a dry \nair mass. Precipitable water values of around 0.50 inches or less \nwhich is about 25% of normal are expected on Friday with PW values \nranging under 0.75 inches on Saturday through Monday. Tuesday and \nWednesday will feature a westerly flow aloft which will maintain a \ndry pattern. An upper level trough spreading across the OH Valley \nand Great Lakes on Wednesday may bring a chance of showers or storms \nto the area late in the work week.",
